WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
HTTPS is the foundation for browser transport protection.

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:08.858
It helps protect data in transit and gives the browser a basis for
stronger security expectations.

3
00:00:08.858 --> 00:00:22.845
Many other controls assume secure transport: Secure cookies, trusted
redirects, service worker behavior, modern browser APIs, and sensitive
form submissions all depend on the application being served over a
properly configured secure channel.

4
00:00:22.845 --> 00:00:26.574
Headers can support this model, but they do not replace TLS itself.

5
00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:34.900
Strict-Transport-Security, commonly called HSTS, tells browsers to use
HTTPS for a site under defined rules after the browser has seen the
policy.

6
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.762
That can help reduce accidental downgrade behavior and mixed transport
assumptions.

7
00:00:39.762 --> 00:00:44.824
HSTS needs careful rollout because browsers remember the policy for the
configured time.

8
00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:55.413
Subdomain coverage and preload concepts can broaden the effect, so teams
should understand domain ownership, legacy hosts, and operational
readiness before using aggressive settings.

9
00:00:55.413 --> 00:01:00.409
Local development, staging, and production often have different transport
realities.

10
00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:05.204
Developers may test with local HTTP while production uses TLS behind a
reverse proxy.

11
00:01:05.204 --> 00:01:11.731
The Python application may see a proxied request and need to understand
forwarded protocol information correctly.

12
00:01:11.731 --> 00:01:20.390
If the application and proxy disagree about whether a request is secure,
cookies, redirects, generated links, and origin checks can behave
incorrectly.

13
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.718
Transport assumptions should be documented and validated in production-
like environments.

14
00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:31.246
Transport-related headers do not replace authentication, authorization, or
input validation.

15
00:01:31.246 --> 00:01:36.041
HTTPS protects the channel; it does not decide whether the user may
perform an action.

16
00:01:36.041 --> 00:01:40.970
HSTS encourages secure transport; it does not fix unsafe templates or
exposed secrets.

17
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:46.831
Secure cookies align session behavior with HTTPS; they do not make session
contents safe by themselves.

18
00:01:46.831 --> 00:01:51.160
Transport controls are essential, but they are one layer in a broader
design.
